.TH CHTTPSVR_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_destroy \- destroy a chttpsvr handle and set it to CHTTPSVR_INVALID
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_destroy(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Destroys the server that name holds, and then sets name to
CHTTPSVR_INVALID.

It drains every in-flight request and closes every idle connection
that remains.  A connection that waits with no thread is ended too: a
parked body or response is closed, a request that waits for body
memory is closed, and a streaming request that waits in the streaming
queue gets 503 Service Unavailable.  It also stops the shared engine
when this is the last active server.  It closes the listener socket as a part
of this, so a
call to chttpsvr_stop first is optional.

A second call on a separate copy of the same handle value is a FATAL
ERROR, and so is a call from inside a request handler or a middleware
of this server.  The second call is fatal both when it is concurrent
and when it comes later, after the first call completes.  The library
reports the error at once with abort() and SIGABRT.  It does this
instead of a silent double-free or a hang.  To shut a server down from
inside one of its own handlers, destroy it from a different thread, or
call chttpsvr_engine_stop().

It is safe to call this macro whether or not the shared engine still
runs.  It frees the engine reference of name, and that can start an
asynchronous engine stop when it was the last reference.  It is also
safe when chttpsvr_engine_stop() already force-stopped the engine
while name was still started.  In that case the engine stop already
quiesced this server.  This call therefore finds the teardown of the
server done, and frees the resources of the server that remain.  This
call waits for that teardown to complete when such a force-stop still
tears this same server down on its own background thread.  It does not
race it.

This macro only STARTS that asynchronous engine stop.  The destroy of
the reactor, the stop of the idle sweep and the close of the
fallback engine logger all run on a separate reaper thread, after this
call returns.  Every guarantee of this call covers name alone: its
listener is closed, its connections are closed, and its worker pool is
drained.  Call chttpsvr_engine_wait() after it whenever that final
teardown must finish before the next step.  Call it before you return
from main().  Call it before you unload this library from an image
that dlopen() loaded.  Call it before you tear
down a custom allocator that chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs()
installed.  The reaper still frees the memory of the reactor and of
the fallback engine logger through that allocator.

The reaper thread itself needs no such call.  An application can
destroy its last server and then call neither chttpsvr_engine_wait()
nor a later chttpsvr_start().  It still leaves no unjoined thread
behind, and no thread stack mapping.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro evaluates name exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
